    "duo_cell_area": {
      "params": [
        "node"
      ],
      "description": "Cell areas on the sphere, shaped [tile, x_cell_ext, y_cell_ext], by the SPHERICAL EXCESS of the geodesic quadrilateral through the cell's four corner nodes (Chen 2021 B8): area = R^2 (a412 + a123 + a234 + a341 - 2 pi), each interior angle supplied by the imported scalar atom ../stencils/duo_spherical_angle.esm. ORIENTATION NOTE: B9 as printed sets a_ijk = acos( (p_i x p_j).(p_j x p_k) / ... ), whose first factor is the arc normal p_i x p_j; combined with B8 that returns the NEGATIVE of the area, under either corner traversal (measured -4 pi R^2 exactly). The angle B8 needs is the polygon's INTERIOR angle at p_j, which is what B9's own prose asks for -- 'the angle between three points with right-hand rule FROM p_j', i.e. both arc normals taken outward from p_j, (p_j x p_i) and (p_j x p_k). The atom implements that reading and the areas come out positive and sum to 4 pi R^2. Traversal order of the four corners is then immaterial (verified: clockwise and counter-clockwise agree bitwise), since reversing it maps each interior angle to itself. This is exact, not an approximation, and it is the natural conformance gate for the whole geometry chain: summed over all 6 N^2 interior cells it reproduces 4 pi R_sphere^2 to 2e-14 at every resolution and projection tested. Bind `node` to duo_node_cart.",
      "body": {
        "op": "aggregate",
        "output_idx": [
          "gt",
          "gi",
          "gj"
